nce these capabilities while tying them to the specific requirement IDs.\n\nNow I'm planning out the four tasks: the first handles the remaining groups requirements (R3.1-R3.3) since the package already exists, the second sets up the env package structure and contract, and I need to define the remaining tasks for printenv and id similarly.\n\nI realize I should check the requirements.yaml file for actual weights and review the SRD to understand what each requirement actually specifies, otherwise my task descriptions will be too vague for the stitch agent to work with effectively.\n\nBut I'm constrained from reading files directly—all the project information should already be in the context provided. So I'll work with what I have and structure the tasks following the pattern I see in the existing commits, where each task references specific requirement IDs from the SRD and the stitch agent handles reading the actual requirements themselves.\n\nLooking at the git history for guidance, the format is consistent: task title includes the SRD identifier, the specific requirements to implement, and a brief phase like \"implementation\" or \"implementation continued\". I'll apply that same structure here for the env requirements.\n\nI'm also noticing the requirement groupings seem to follow a logical progression—R1.x for core functionality, R2.x for flags and options, R3.x for error handling or remaining features. For env specifically, the later requirements likely cover command execution and edge cases, while for groups the remaining work is probably error handling or additional features.\n\nFor the contract task, I'm reconsidering whether a cmd/ package really needs one since these are typically just thin wrappers around the main logic. But I'll propose it anyway and let the team decide if it's necessary. Looking at the package_contracts field, I see contracts are only defined for pkg/ packages, not cmd/ packages, which suggests the contract requirement applies specifically to library packages that have external consumers. Since cmd/ packages are implementation-focused with no exported APIs for other packages to depend on, I'll proceed with implementation tasks for the new cmd/env package without a contract task first.\n\nNow I'm checking if I can pack the requirements more efficiently—each task allows up to 4 sub-requirements with a maximum weight of 4, so I'm seeing if I can combine more requirements per task or if the current grouping of 3 requirements each is already optimal.\n\nLooking at the alternative packing where I mix requirement groups across tasks—putting env R1.1-R1.3 and R2.1 together, then R2.2-R2.3 with R3.1-R3.2, and finally R3.3 with R4.1-R4.3—this would actually complete all of env in this batch, which is more efficient than spreading it across multiple batches.\n\nI'm also noticing from the previous task pattern that mixing requirement groups within a single task is acceptable, so this approach should work. 1, R3.2 (implementation continued)**\n- 4 requirements, weight 4\n- Depends on task 2\n\nThis approach fits 4 requirements per task where possible, completing the environment requirements group while leaving R4.1-R4.3 for the next batch.\n\nNow I'm writing out the actual task descriptions as YAML documents. For the groups work, I need to build on the existing package that already has R1.1-R2.3 implemented, focusing on R3 which covers error handling and edge cases. For the environment package, I'm creating a new cmd/env/ directory from scratch, pulling in knowledge from the SRD and the existing system package for signal handling.\n\nLooking at what these utilities actually do: env modifies the environment and runs commands with those modifications—it can set variables, unset them, start with a clean slate, and supports various output formats. The other utilities like printenv, id, and groups handle printing environment variables, user/group information, and group memberships respectively.\n\nFor the task descriptions, I need to make sure the stitch agent has enough context to work independently. Each task should reference the SRD, the existing implementation patterns from the codebase, and testing utilities. The standard approach here is to include the relevant SRD file, the ARCHITECTURE.yaml for project conventions, any existing code being modified, and the testing patterns from pkg/testutils and the SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys. For the cmd/ packages specifically, I'm looking at main.go for the implementation and the corresponding _test.go file for differential tests.\n\nSince cmd/ packages don't have formal contracts defined in package_contracts, I'll skip contract-related tasks for those. For the env package as a new cmd/ implementation, the first task focuses on creating main.go. I'm checking the weight totals to ensure each task stays within the limit—R3.1, R3.2, and R3.3 from groups each have weight 1, totaling 3, which fits the constraint. For env, R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R2.1 also need to be verified for their combined weight.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML task descriptions with specific fields like deliverable_type set to code, required_reading listing the relevant files, files with their actions, requirements with IDs and descriptions, and acceptance criteria to validate completion.\n\nSince I can't directly read the SRD, I'll reference the requirements by their IDs and craft descriptions based on what the stitch agent should implement for each utility, making sure the task instructions are clear enough for the agent to work from the SRD files themselves.\n\nFor **groups**, R3 likely covers error handling, diagnostics, and help functionality. For **env**, the requirements probably extend further—R3 might handle command execution and special flags like splitting strings or changing directories, while R4 would cover error handling and version information.\n\nRather than trying to guess the exact structure, I'll write task descriptions that reference the specific SRD requirement IDs and let the agent consult the actual SRD for the details, while I describe the general scope of what each requirement group should accomplish.\n\nNow I'm drafting the task descriptions, starting with groups R3.1 through R3.3, which will include the required reading list pointing to the SRD, the existing implementation, test patterns, and signal handling utilities, along with the file modifications needed. I need to note that the SIGP handler installation should remain in place at the start of main, then move on to the next set of requirements covering the env task with R1.1 through R2.1. I'm laying out the remaining requirements for R1.2, R1.3, and R2.1, along with design decisions around SIGPIPE handling, differential testing with a reference binary, and locale configuration for test consistency. Now I'm looking at the next phase of the env implementation, which requires adding support for R2.2, R2.3, R3.1, and R3.2 requirements from the SRD. I need to modify the main implementation and expand the test suite with differential tests to verify the new functionality works correctly against the reference binary. Now I'm looking at the requirements for the next phase—I need to implement R3.3 through R4.3 by extending the existing env command, and I'll need to add corresponding differential tests using the reference binary to validate the new functionality. I'm verifying the dependency chain works correctly—Task 0 is independent, Task 1 creates the new package, Task 2 builds on it, and Task 3 depends on Task 2's completion. Let me check if Tasks 0 and 1 could run in parallel since they don't depend on each other.\n\nNow I need to apply the canonical ordering constraint: documentation before code, contracts before implementations, types before consumers, implementation before tests. Both are code with no contracts needed, so I should order by file path alphabetically—cmd/env comes before cmd/groups. But groups is partially done while env is new, so I'm